A **collapsible list** (accordion/tree) toggles visibility of nested content while keeping the DOM lean and state predictable. The interview task tests controlled state, recursive rendering, and basic accessibility.

## Mental model

Think of each row as a node with `id`, `label`, `children`, and an **expanded** flag. Clicking a parent toggles only that node's expanded state — children render when expanded.

## Implementation sketch

```javascript
function TreeNode({ node, expanded, onToggle }) {
  const hasChildren = node.children?.length > 0;
  const isOpen = expanded.has(node.id);

  return (
    <div role="treeitem" aria-expanded={hasChildren ? isOpen : undefined}>
      <button type="button" onClick={() => hasChildren && onToggle(node.id)}>
        {hasChildren ? (isOpen ? "▼" : "▶") : "•"} {node.label}
      </button>
      {isOpen && hasChildren && (
        <div role="group">
          {node.children.map((child) => (
            <TreeNode
              key={child.id}
              node={child}
              expanded={expanded}
              onToggle={onToggle}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

function CollapsibleList({ data }) {
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(new Set());

  const onToggle = (id) => {
    setExpanded((prev) => {
      const next = new Set(prev);
      next.has(id) ? next.delete(id) : next.add(id);
      return next;
    });
  };

  return (
    <div role="tree">
      {data.map((node) => (
        <TreeNode
          key={node.id}
          node={node}
          expanded={expanded}
          onToggle={onToggle}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Design choices

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Set` of expanded ids | O(1) toggle, easy multi-open | Slightly more state logic |
| Single `openId` (accordion) | Simple | Only one section open |
| Store expanded on tree data | Colocated | Mutating tree or deep updates |

## How to answer in the interview

> "I'd use a Set of expanded node IDs in state, recursive TreeNode components, and toggle on click. I'd add aria-expanded and role=tree for accessibility. Keys on children use stable ids."

## Common mistakes

- Using array index as `key` for dynamic trees
- Forgetting `aria-expanded` on interactive parents
- Re-rendering entire tree on every toggle without memoization on large trees

## Key takeaways

- Controlled `expanded` state + recursive render pattern.
- `Set` for efficient toggle; `role="tree"` / `aria-expanded` for a11y.
- Distinguish accordion (single open) vs multi-expand list.

## Interview follow-ups

- **Animate expand/collapse?** — CSS `max-height` + transition, or `height: auto` with ref measurement.
- **Lazy-load children?** — Fetch on first expand, show skeleton while loading.

Before JSX and ES6 classes, React components were written in **ES5** using `React.createClass` or manual constructor functions with `prototype`. Building a **mini React-like class system** tests your understanding of `this`, prototypes, state, and lifecycle — without framework magic.

## Minimal Component base (ES5)

```javascript
function Component(props) {
  this.props = props || {};
  this.state = {};
}

Component.prototype.setState = function (partial, callback) {
  for (var key in partial) {
    if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(partial, key)) {
      this.state[key] = partial[key];
    }
  }
  this.forceUpdate(callback);
};

Component.prototype.forceUpdate = function (callback) {
  if (typeof this._onUpdate === "function") {
    this._onUpdate(this);
  }
  if (typeof callback === "function") callback();
};
```

## Counter component

```javascript
function Counter(props) {
  Component.call(this, props);
  this.state = { count: 0 };
}
Counter.prototype = Object.create(Component.prototype);
Counter.prototype.constructor = Counter;

Counter.prototype.increment = function () {
  this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
};

Counter.prototype.render = function () {
  return {
    type: "div",
    props: {
      children: [
        "Count: " + this.state.count,
        {
          type: "button",
          props: { onClick: this.increment.bind(this), children: "+" },
        },
      ],
    },
  };
};
```

## Lifecycle hooks (sketch)

```javascript
function mountComponent(instance, container) {
  if (instance.componentWillMount) instance.componentWillMount();
  var vnode = instance.render();
  container.appendChild(renderVNode(vnode));
  if (instance.componentDidMount) instance.componentDidMount();
  instance._onUpdate = function () {
    container.innerHTML = "";
    container.appendChild(renderVNode(instance.render()));
  };
}
```

## ES6 class equivalent

```javascript
class Counter extends React.Component {
  state = { count: 0 };
  increment = () => this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
  render() {
    return <div>{this.state.count}</div>;
  }
}
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "ES5 components use constructor + prototype inheritance from a base Component. setState merges partial state and triggers re-render via forceUpdate. render returns a virtual tree. I'd wire componentWillMount/DidMount around mount for lifecycle parity."

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting `Object.create` for prototype chain
- Not binding event handlers (`this` loss)
- Mutating state directly instead of setState merge

## Key takeaways

- `Component` base provides `props`, `state`, `setState`, `forceUpdate`.
- Prototype inheritance replaces `class extends`.
- `render` returns description; host code mounts/diffs.

## Interview follow-ups

- **Why bind in constructor?** — Stable reference vs inline arrow in ES6.
- **How does real React batch setState?** — Queues updates, merges, single re-render.

It is no coincidence that React developers fall in love with closures — or get utterly confused by them. Under the hood, React Hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, `useCallback`, etc.) rely entirely on **closures** and **lexical environments** to persist state between re-renders.

For general closure theory (definition, `var` loop trap, memory leaks), see JS Learnings **#130 Closures** and **#129 Execution Context**.

## How `useState` works using a closure

Have you ever wondered how a local variable inside a functional component doesn't get wiped out on every re-render?

Conceptually, React keeps an array of state slots outside your component. The `setState` function is a **closure** — it captures the hook index so it always knows which slot to update.

Simplified blueprint:

```javascript
const React = (function () {
  let memory = [];
  let index = 0;

  return {
    render(Component) {
      index = 0;
      const Comp = Component();
      return Comp;
    },
    useState(initialValue) {
      const currentIndex = index;

      if (memory[currentIndex] === undefined) {
        memory[currentIndex] = initialValue;
      }

      const setState = (newValue) => {
        memory[currentIndex] =
          typeof newValue === "function"
            ? newValue(memory[currentIndex])
            : newValue;
      };

      const currentState = memory[currentIndex];
      index++;
      return [currentState, setState];
    },
  };
})();
```

Because `setState` closes over `currentIndex`, no matter where you call that setter later, it always knows exactly which slot in React's memory array it belongs to.

## The stale closure trap

This is the single most common bug in modern React. A **stale closure** happens when a function captures variables from a past render and refuses to see updated values on future renders.

```javascript
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      // BUG: stale closure!
      console.log(`Current count is: ${count}`);
      setCount(count + 1);
    }, 1000);

    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []); // runs once — locks in Render #1's `count`

  return <div>{count}</div>;
}
```

**What happens:**

1. **Render #1:** `count` is `0`. `useEffect` runs once and creates a `setInterval` callback that closes over `count === 0`.
2. The interval ticks → logs `Current count is: 0` → calls `setCount(0 + 1)`.
3. **Render #2:** `count` is now `1`.
4. **The trap:** The old interval callback is still locked inside Render #1's closure. It still sees `count` as `0`. Every tick does `0 + 1`, locking the UI at `1`.

## Fix: functional updates

Never read state directly inside asynchronous or delayed callbacks when the effect has a stale dependency array. Pass a **callback** to the setter — React hands you the latest state:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => {
    setCount((prevCount) => prevCount + 1);
  }, 1000);

  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, []);
```

`prevCount` is always the freshest value — no stale closure.

## Alternative fix: dependency array

Add `count` to the effect deps so the interval is torn down and recreated when `count` changes:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, [count]);
```

This works but re-subscribes every render when `count` changes — functional updates are usually cleaner for timers and polling.

## Key takeaway

> React hooks are closures. State survives re-renders because setters close over hook slots. Stale closures happen when async code captures an old render's variables — fix with functional updaters (`setState(prev => ...)`) or correct dependency arrays.

## Interview one-liner

> "`useState` setters are closures over React's internal hook index. A stale closure is when a `useEffect` callback from an old render still reads old state — fix it with functional updates or by listing the variable in the dependency array."

**StrictMode** is a development-only wrapper (`<React.StrictMode>`) that helps surface unsafe lifecycles, legacy APIs, and side effects that break React's assumptions.

## What it does in development

| Behavior | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| **Double-invokes** render functions | Catch impure render logic (mutations, random values) |
| **Double-invokes** effects (mount → unmount → remount) | Verify cleanup functions run correctly |
| Warns about deprecated APIs | `findDOMNode`, legacy context, string refs |
| Detects unexpected side effects | Logic that should live in `useEffect` but runs during render |

## React 18 mount/unmount/remount cycle

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('mounted');
  return () => console.log('cleanup');
}, []);
// StrictMode dev logs: mounted → cleanup → mounted
```

Production builds **do not** double-render — only development.

## Impure logic it catches

```javascript
// BAD — new array every render, breaks memoization downstream
function List() {
  const items = generateItems(); // impure / unstable reference
  return items.map((i) => <Row key={i.id} data={i} />);
}

// GOOD — stable state or memoized value
const [items, setItems] = useState(() => generateItems());
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "StrictMode is dev-only. It double-renders and double-mounts effects to expose missing cleanups and impure render logic. It does not affect production behavior."

## Key takeaways

- Never rely on effects running only once in development.
- Write idempotent effects with proper cleanup.
- Impure renders (mutations, random IDs) show up immediately under StrictMode.

A **pure function** always returns the same output for the same inputs and causes **no side effects** (no network calls, no DOM writes, no mutating arguments).

## Pure function definition

```javascript
// Pure — same inputs → same output, no side effects
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

// Impure — mutates external state
let total = 0;
function addToTotal(n) {
  total += n;
  return total;
}

// Impure — depends on external mutable state
function getTimestamp() {
  return Date.now();
}
```

## Pure components

A React component is **pure** when it renders the same UI given the same props and state — no side effects during render.

| API | Mechanism |
| --- | --- |
| `React.PureComponent` (class) | Shallow-compare props + state before re-render |
| `React.memo` (function) | Shallow-compare props before re-render |

```javascript
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
});
```

## Why purity matters for reconciliation

React assumes renders are **idempotent** — it may call your component multiple times (StrictMode, concurrent rendering). Side effects in render cause bugs that are hard to reproduce.

## Key takeaways

- Keep render logic pure: compute UI from props/state only.
- Side effects belong in `useEffect`, event handlers, or server actions.
- `React.memo` is the function-component equivalent of `PureComponent`.

Both hooks run **after** React commits DOM changes, but at different points relative to the browser paint.

## Timing comparison

| | `useLayoutEffect` | `useEffect` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| When | Synchronously after DOM mutation, **before paint** | Asynchronously **after paint** |
| Blocks paint? | Yes | No |
| Runs on server? | No (warns in SSR) | No |
| Typical use | DOM measurements, synchronous layout fixes | Data fetching, subscriptions, logging |

## Render → commit → effects pipeline

```
Render → DOM commit → useLayoutEffect → browser paint → useEffect
```

## useLayoutEffect — prevent visual flicker

```javascript
function Tooltip({ anchorRef }) {
  const tooltipRef = useRef(null);
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const anchor = anchorRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    const tooltip = tooltipRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    setPos({
      x: anchor.left + anchor.width / 2 - tooltip.width / 2,
      y: anchor.bottom + 8,
    });
  }, [anchorRef]);

  return <div ref={tooltipRef} style={{ position: 'fixed', left: pos.x, top: pos.y }} />;
}
```

If this measurement ran in `useEffect`, the user would see the tooltip jump from `(0,0)` to the correct position.

## useEffect — default for side effects

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
}, [count]);

useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal });
  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

## Rule of thumb

> Start with `useEffect`. Reach for `useLayoutEffect` only when you measure or mutate layout and users see a flash.

## Key takeaways

- `useLayoutEffect` = synchronous, blocks paint, layout-critical.
- `useEffect` = non-blocking, preferred for most side effects.
- Both cleanups run before the next invocation and on unmount.

**React 18** introduced a concurrent renderer that can **pause, resume, and prioritize** work instead of blocking the main thread until a full tree is finished.

## React 18 highlights

| Feature | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `createRoot` | New root API (replaces `ReactDOM.render`) enabling concurrent features |
| Automatic batching | Multiple `setState` calls in timeouts, promises, and native handlers batch into one render |
| `useTransition` | Mark state updates as non-urgent (interruptible) |
| `useDeferredValue` | Defer a value until urgent work finishes |
| `Suspense` + `lazy` | Code-split components; show fallback while loading |
| Streaming SSR | Send HTML in chunks (with frameworks like Next.js) |

```javascript
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';

const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(<App />);
```

## Concurrent rendering mental model

React can keep **two versions** of the UI in memory — the current committed tree and a work-in-progress tree. Urgent updates (typing in an input) can interrupt a slow re-render (filtering a huge list).

## useTransition

```javascript
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

function handleSearch(query) {
  setInputValue(query); // urgent — update input immediately

  startTransition(() => {
    setResults(expensiveFilter(allItems, query)); // non-urgent — can be interrupted
  });
}
```

## useDeferredValue

Use when you **receive** a value you don't control (e.g. a prop):

```javascript
const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;
const results = useMemo(() => filter(items, deferredQuery), [items, deferredQuery]);
```

## Suspense + lazy

```javascript
const HeavyChart = React.lazy(() => import('./HeavyChart'));

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
      <HeavyChart />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

The fallback shows while the dynamic `import()` promise is pending.

## Key takeaways

- Concurrent = interruptible render phase; commit is still atomic.
- `useTransition` for updates you control; `useDeferredValue` for values you receive.
- Automatic batching applies everywhere in React 18 — use `flushSync` only when you need immediate DOM updates.

Hooks rely on a **stable call order** across renders. React stores hook state in an internal array indexed by call position.

## The two rules

1. **Only call hooks at the top level** — never inside conditions, loops, or nested functions.
2. **Only call hooks from React function components or custom hooks** — not from regular JS functions, class components, or event handlers.

## Why order matters

```javascript
// WRONG — conditional hook breaks call order between renders
function Bad({ show }) {
  if (show) {
    const [name, setName] = useState(''); // hook index shifts!
  }
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
}

// CORRECT — hooks always run in the same order
function Good({ show }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  if (!show) return null;
  return <div>{name} {count}</div>;
}
```

## Valid vs invalid

| Valid | Invalid |
| --- | --- |
| Top-level in component body | `if (x) useState()` |
| Inside custom hook (`useAuth`) | Inside `forEach` / `map` |
| | Inside event handler callback |
| | Inside class component |

## ESLint enforcement

`eslint-plugin-react-hooks` (`rules-of-hooks`, `exhaustive-deps`) catches most violations at build time.

## Key takeaways

- Hooks are linked-list slots — position must be identical every render.
- Extract conditional logic **after** hooks, or into separate components.
- Custom hooks can call other hooks; plain helpers cannot.

`React.memo` wraps a component and **skips re-rendering** when props are shallowly equal to the previous render.

## Basic usage

```javascript
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item} onClick={() => onSelect(item)}>{item}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
});
```

## The reference trap

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const data = { name: 'Alice' }; // new object every render!

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Child data={data} /> {/* memo is useless — new reference each time */}
    </>
  );
}

// Fix: stabilize with useMemo
const data = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'Alice' }), []);
```

## Custom comparison function

Second argument: `(prevProps, nextProps) => boolean` — return **`true` to SKIP** re-render (opposite of `shouldComponentUpdate`).

```javascript
const OptimizedList = React.memo(
  function OptimizedList({ items }) {
    return <ul>{items.map((i) => <li key={i.id}>{i.name}</li>)}</ul>;
  },
  (prev, next) => prev.items.length === next.items.length
);
```

Use custom comparators sparingly — shallow compare is correct for most cases.

## When memo helps

- Component is **measurably expensive** to render
- Props are **stable** (primitives, memoized objects/callbacks)
- Parent re-renders frequently with unchanged child props

## Key takeaways

- Default: shallow prop comparison.
- Custom comparator returns `true` = skip render.
- Pair with `useMemo` / `useCallback` to stabilize object and function props.

**Profile first, optimize second.** React DevTools Profiler shows which components render and how long they take.

## Step-by-step playbook

| Step | Action |
| --- | --- |
| 1. Measure | React DevTools Profiler, browser Performance tab |
| 2. Reduce unnecessary renders | `React.memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback` |
| 3. Virtualize long lists | `react-window`, `@tanstack/react-virtual` (see Learnings #4) |
| 4. Code-split routes | `React.lazy` + `Suspense` |
| 5. Defer non-urgent work | `useTransition`, `useDeferredValue` |
| 6. Fix context | Split contexts, memoize Provider value |
| 7. Move work off main thread | Web Workers for heavy computation (rare) |

## Quick wins

```javascript
// Stable callback for memoized child
const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {
  setSelected(id);
}, []);

// Memoize expensive derived data
const sorted = useMemo(() => heavySort(items), [items]);

// Defer heavy filter during typing
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
const filtered = useMemo(() => filter(all, deferredQuery), [all, deferredQuery]);
```

## Anti-patterns

- Memoizing every component (overhead > benefit for cheap trees)
- `useMemo` on values that change every render anyway
- Putting high-frequency state (scroll, mouse) in Context

## How to answer in the interview

> "I'd profile with React DevTools, identify expensive or unnecessary renders, then apply targeted fixes — memo, virtualization, code splitting, and context splitting. I avoid premature optimization."

## Key takeaways

- Measure before memoizing.
- Virtualization for long lists; lazy loading for routes.
- Context and unstable references are the most common hidden causes.

Context has **no selective subscription** — every consumer re-renders when the Provider's `value` changes, even if the consumer only reads one field.

## The problem

```javascript
const AppContext = createContext({ user: null, theme: 'light', cart: [] });

function UserName() {
  const { user } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>{user?.name}</span>;
  // Re-renders when theme OR cart changes — not just user
}
```

## Fix 1: Split contexts by update frequency

```javascript
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
const UserContext = createContext(null);
const CartContext = createContext([]);

// UserName only subscribes to UserContext
```

## Fix 2: Memoize the Provider value

```javascript
function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');

  const value = useMemo(
    () => ({ user, theme, setUser, setTheme }),
    [user, theme]
  );

  return <AppContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AppContext.Provider>;
}
```

Without `useMemo`, `value={{ user, theme }}` creates a new object every parent render → all consumers re-render.

## Fix 3: Split state and dispatch

```javascript
const StateContext = createContext(null);
const DispatchContext = createContext(null);

// Dispatch context value is stable (same dispatch function)
// Components that only call dispatch don't re-render on state changes
```

## Fix 4: External store with selectors

For high-frequency or granular updates, use **Zustand**, **Jotai**, or **Redux** with selectors instead of raw Context.

## Key takeaways

- Context is for low-frequency, cross-cutting data (theme, auth, locale).
- Never put rapidly changing values (scroll position, keystrokes) in Context.
- Split + memoize Provider values before reaching for external state libraries.

By default, **refs do not pass through** function components. `forwardRef` lets a component accept a ref from its parent and attach it to an inner DOM node or imperative handle.

## Basic pattern

```javascript
const Input = React.forwardRef(function Input(props, ref) {
  return <input {...props} ref={ref} />;
});

function Form() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  const focusInput = () => inputRef.current?.focus();

  return (
    <>
      <Input ref={inputRef} placeholder="Email" />
      <button type="button" onClick={focusInput}>Focus</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

## useImperativeHandle — custom ref API

Expose a limited imperative API instead of the raw DOM node:

```javascript
const FancyInput = React.forwardRef(function FancyInput(props, ref) {
  const innerRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus: () => innerRef.current?.focus(),
    clear: () => { if (innerRef.current) innerRef.current.value = ''; },
  }));

  return <input ref={innerRef} {...props} />;
});
```

## When you need it

- Focus management (modals, form libraries)
- Measuring DOM dimensions
- Integrating with non-React libraries that need a DOM node
- Design-system primitives (`Button`, `Input`) that parents must control

## Key takeaways

- `forwardRef(Component)` receives `(props, ref)`.
- Prefer declarative props over refs when possible.
- `useImperativeHandle` limits what parents can do imperatively.

Accessible React apps work for keyboard, screen reader, and assistive-tech users — not just mouse users.

## Core practices

| Practice | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Semantic HTML | `<button>` not `<div onClick>` |
| Labels | `<label htmlFor={id}>` paired with `<input id={id}>` |
| ARIA when semantics aren't enough | `aria-expanded`, `role="dialog"`, `aria-live` |
| Keyboard navigation | Tab order, Enter/Space activation, Escape to close |
| Focus management | Move focus into modals, return on close |

## React-specific patterns

```javascript
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, children }) {
  const dialogRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen) {
      dialogRef.current?.focus();
      const onKey = (e) => e.key === 'Escape' && onClose();
      document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);
      return () => document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
    }
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return createPortal(
    <div role="dialog" aria-modal="true" ref={dialogRef} tabIndex={-1}>
      {children}
    </div>,
    document.getElementById('modal-root')
  );
}
```

## useId for stable associations

```javascript
function Field({ label }) {
  const id = useId();
  return (
    <>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>
      <input id={id} />
    </>
  );
}
```

`useId` generates SSR-safe unique IDs — use for `htmlFor` / `aria-labelledby`, not list keys.

## Libraries and testing

- **Radix UI**, **Headless UI**, **React Aria** — accessible primitives
- **eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y** — lint-time checks
- **axe-core**, **jest-axe** — automated tests
- Manual testing with VoiceOver / NVDA

## Key takeaways

- Start with semantic HTML; add ARIA only when needed.
- Manage focus for modals, route changes, and dynamic content.
- Test with keyboard-only navigation and a screen reader.

Not all logic belongs in a custom hook. **Hooks call hooks**; plain helpers do pure computation. Mixing them incorrectly causes bugs or unnecessary coupling.

## The useTasks pattern

```javascript
// Pure helper — no hooks, easy to unit test
function generateRandomTasks(count) {
  const verbs = ['Write', 'Review', 'Fix', 'Deploy'];
  return Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => ({
    id: crypto.randomUUID(),
    title: `${verbs[i % verbs.length]} task ${i + 1}`,
    done: false,
  }));
}

// Custom hook — owns React state and effects
function useTasks(initialCount = 5) {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState(() => generateRandomTasks(initialCount));

  const addTask = useCallback((title) => {
    setTasks((prev) => [...prev, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), title, done: false }]);
  }, []);

  const toggleTask = useCallback((id) => {
    setTasks((prev) =>
      prev.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t))
    );
  }, []);

  const regenerate = useCallback(() => {
    setTasks(generateRandomTasks(initialCount));
  }, [initialCount]);

  return { tasks, addTask, toggleTask, regenerate };
}
```

## Decision guide

| Put in custom hook | Put in plain helper |
| --- | --- |
| Uses `useState`, `useEffect`, etc. | Pure data transformation |
| Needs lifecycle or subscriptions | Formatting, validation, sorting |
| Returns state + actions together | Shared between hook and non-React code |

## Anti-pattern: helper that secretly needs hooks

```javascript
// WRONG — can't call useState inside a regular function
function useTasksHelper() {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([]); // only valid in hooks!
}

// CORRECT — rename to useTasks and call from components only
```

## Key takeaways

- Extract **pure logic** to helpers; keep **stateful orchestration** in hooks.
- Custom hooks compose other hooks; helpers are framework-agnostic.
- Helpers are trivially testable without `renderHook`.
